>> title: Borscht, Instant Pot
>> category: Soups
>> tags: beets, beef, cabbage, ukrainian, winter, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> aka: instant pot borscht, pressure cooker borscht, instant pot beet soup, borsch, borshch, barszcz, beet soup, red borscht, Ukrainian borscht
>> servings: 6
>> dish: borscht
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> time: 2 hr
>> step.1: brown the short ribs on Sauté 10 min
>> step.2: fry the onion and carrot in the fat 8 min
>> step.3: stock in, ribs back — pressure cook 40 min
>> step.4: natural release 15 min, pull the meat off the bones
>> step.5: beets, potato and cabbage, lid off, 20 min
>> step.6: season, sour cream at the table

Brown @beef short ribs{2%lb}(900 g; bone-in) in the #Instant Pot{} on the highest Sauté setting in their own fat ~brown{10%min}, in two batches, and lift them out. Two batches is not fussiness — the pot's element is small and narrow, and three pounds of beef crowded into it steams grey instead of browning. If you have a wide skillet, it will do this better; the pot's argument is that it is one pot to wash.

Fry @yellow onion{1%large}(chopped) and @carrot{1%large}(grated) in @unsalted butter{2%Tbs} in the same pot ~fry{8%min}, scraping the brown from the bottom as they go. Scrape it properly: a pot with anything stuck to the base will read a burn and refuse to come to pressure.

Return @&(~2)short ribs{} to @&(~1)vegetables{} with @beef stock{6%cups}(1.4 L), lock the lid and ~pressure cook{40%min} at high pressure. Forty minutes is the short rib's own figure under pressure, not the ninety-minute simmer scaled — bone-in short rib is collagen, and collagen goes at its own pace whatever pot it is in.

Let @&(~1)pot{} ~natural release{15%min}, then pull the meat off the bones and discard them. A natural release on meat is not waiting, it is cooking: the temperature falls slowly enough that the rib keeps going soft. Vent it early and the meat tightens.

Simmer @&(~1)pot{} with the lid off on Sauté ~simmer{20%min} with @beets{1 1/2%lb}(700 g; peeled and grated), @tomato paste{2%Tbs}, @cider vinegar{2%Tbs}, @russet potatoes{3/4%lb}(340 g; cubed) and @green cabbage{3%cups}(shredded). None of these go under pressure. Grated beet under a lid turns brown and sweet instead of red and earthy, and cabbage collapses to threads — the vinegar in with them here is what holds the colour. The clock barely moves against the stovetop version; what moves is that fifty-five of these minutes are the lid locked and you are elsewhere.

Season @&(~1)borscht{} with @garlic{2%cloves}(minced), @kosher salt{2%tsp} and @fresh dill{3%Tbs}(chopped), and pass @sour cream{2/3%cup}(160 g) at the table.
